\verb{files/<data id>/<base name>} in the copy that is served (so the served
page never holds the local path); the scene itself is not changed. Tile
blobs of embedded layers are served as blobs, so one scene can mix
embedded and served layers.

\strong{Routes}, all under \verb{http://127.0.0.1:<port>/<token>/}: the page (\code{""}
and \code{index.html}), \code{aob-renderer.min.js}, \verb{blob/<encoded key>} and
\verb{files/<data id>/<base name>}. \verb{/<token>} answers 301 to \verb{/<token>/};
anything else, including a wrong token, answers 404. Only \code{GET} and
\code{HEAD} are answered (405 otherwise). A file answers a single
\verb{Range: bytes=} request with 206 and those bytes (a range past the end, a
multi-range request or one longer than \code{getOption("aobcore.serve_range_max", 64 * 2^20)} bytes answers 416), and a request without \code{Range} with the
whole file. A registered file that has changed since it was registered
answers 409, and one that is gone 404, each with a warning in R.

\strong{Security.} The server binds the loopback interface only. Every route
is under a random 128-bit token, read from \verb{/dev/urandom} on Unix-alikes
(Linux, macOS). Elsewhere (Windows) a weaker fallback hashes the time,
process id and similar values, and says so in a message: that token
guards against other web pages but does not resist other users of the
same computer, who can narrow those inputs down. The port is drawn from
separate random bytes, between 20000 and 60000; with \verb{/dev/urandom} it
says nothing about the token, but with the fallback both come from the
same guessable inputs, so a visible port helps guess the token. Neither
uses R's random number generator, and \code{.Random.seed} is restored around
httpuv (which draws from it), so it is untouched. A request whose \code{Host}
is not \verb{127.0.0.1:<port>} or \verb{localhost:<port>} is refused (403, with a
warning once per distinct \code{Host}, for the first 5 per server, then once
to say further refusals are not shown) unless listed in
\code{getOption("aobcore.serve_hosts")}, for an IDE proxy that forwards
requests with its own \code{Host}. There are no CORS headers, responses carry
\code{X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff}, the page sends no referrer, and only
the registered files can be read. The token is not authentication:
anyone who sees the URL can read the scene while it is served.

\strong{Selections} (decision 0007). With the 'jsonlite' package, the server
also takes a websocket at \verb{/<token>/ws}, through which the page sends the
viewer's selection and settled view back to R; see \link{serve_scene_socket}
for the messages, the checks and the handle's \code{selection()},
\code{view_state()}, \code{wait()}, \code{on()} and \code{connections()}. \code{select} names the
layers the page lets the viewer select. Without 'jsonlite' the page is
served as before, with no socket, and \code{serve_scene()} says so once per
session.

